JAVA ENGINEERING REFERENCE
Core Java, Multithreading, Advanced Java, Spring Boot and Spring AI

==================================================
1. CORE JAVA
==================================================

1.1 Object-Oriented Programming

Java is an object-oriented programming language based on four major principles:

1. Encapsulation
2. Inheritance
3. Polymorphism
4. Abstraction

Encapsulation protects object state by controlling access to fields through methods.

Inheritance allows a class to reuse behavior from another class.

Polymorphism allows the same interface or parent type to represent different implementations.

Abstraction exposes essential behavior while hiding implementation details.

1.2 Collections

The Java Collections Framework provides data structures including:

List:
- ArrayList
- LinkedList

Set:
- HashSet
- LinkedHashSet
- TreeSet

Map:
- HashMap
- LinkedHashMap
- TreeMap

ArrayList provides efficient indexed access. HashMap provides average constant-time lookup for get and put operations when hash distribution is good.

1.3 Exception Handling

Java provides structured exception handling using:

try
catch
finally
throw
throws

Checked exceptions are verified by the compiler. RuntimeException and its subclasses are unchecked exceptions.

==================================================
2. MULTITHREADING
==================================================

2.1 Thread

A Thread represents an independent execution path.

Runnable represents a task without a return value.

Callable represents a task that can return a value and throw an exception.

2.2 ExecutorService

ExecutorService manages a pool of worker threads.

Example:

ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

executor.submit(() -> {
    System.out.println("Executing task");
});

executor.shutdown();

Using ExecutorService is generally preferable to creating large numbers of threads manually.

2.3 synchronized

The synchronized keyword provides mutual exclusion.

Only one thread can execute a synchronized section guarded by the same monitor at a time.

Synchronization also provides memory visibility guarantees.

2.4 volatile

The volatile keyword guarantees visibility of changes between threads.

However, volatile does not make compound operations atomic.

For example:

counter++;

is not atomic even when counter is volatile.

2.5 AtomicInteger

AtomicInteger provides atomic operations such as incrementAndGet and compareAndSet.

2.6 CompletableFuture

CompletableFuture supports asynchronous programming.

Important methods include:

thenApply
thenCompose
thenCombine
exceptionally
handle

thenCompose is useful when one asynchronous operation depends on the result of another asynchronous operation.

==================================================
3. ADVANCED JAVA
==================================================

3.1 Streams

Streams provide a declarative approach to collection processing.

Common operations include:

filter
map
flatMap
sorted
distinct
collect
reduce

Example:

List<String> result = names.stream()
        .filter(name -> name.startsWith("A"))
        .map(String::toUpperCase)
        .toList();

3.2 Generics

Generics provide compile-time type safety.

Example:

List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

The wildcard ? extends T is commonly used when reading from a producer.

The wildcard ? super T is commonly used when writing to a consumer.

3.3 Reflection

Reflection allows a program to inspect classes, constructors, methods, fields, and annotations at runtime.

Reflection is heavily used by frameworks such as Spring.

3.4 JVM

The JVM executes Java bytecode.

Important runtime memory areas include:

Heap
Stack
Metaspace
Program Counter
Native Method Stack

Garbage collection automatically reclaims objects that are no longer reachable.

==================================================
4. SPRING BOOT
==================================================

4.1 Dependency Injection

Dependency injection allows an object to receive dependencies from the Spring container.

Constructor injection is generally preferred because required dependencies are explicit and can be final.

Example:

@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository repository;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

4.2 Spring Components

Common Spring stereotype annotations include:

@Component
@Service
@Repository
@Controller
@RestController

4.3 REST APIs

Spring Boot supports REST APIs through annotations such as:

@GetMapping
@PostMapping
@PutMapping
@DeleteMapping

Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public Order getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return service.findById(id);
    }
}

4.4 Configuration

Spring Boot applications commonly use:

application.properties

or

application.yml

Configuration can also be bound to Java objects using @ConfigurationProperties.

==================================================
5. SPRING AI
==================================================

5.1 Retrieval Augmented Generation

RAG stands for Retrieval Augmented Generation.

A typical RAG pipeline contains:

1. Document ingestion
2. Document extraction
3. Document transformation
4. Text chunking
5. Embedding generation
6. Vector storage
7. Similarity search
8. Context construction
9. LLM generation

5.2 Spring AI Document

Spring AI represents extracted content using the Document abstraction.

A Document generally contains text content and metadata.

Useful metadata can include:

source
fileName
pageNumber
documentType
createdAt

5.3 Document Processing

A document ingestion pipeline can be modeled as:

Extractor -> Transformer -> Loader

The extractor reads the source file.

The transformer converts and chunks the extracted documents.

The loader persists the resulting documents or embeddings.

5.4 TokenTextSplitter

TokenTextSplitter can divide large documents into smaller chunks before embedding.

A reasonable starting configuration for a general RAG application is approximately 800 tokens per chunk with a minimum chunk size around 350 characters.

Chunk size should be evaluated against the embedding model, document structure, retrieval quality, and LLM context window.

5.5 Vector Store

A vector store stores embeddings and associated document metadata.

During retrieval, a query is converted into an embedding and compared with stored vectors.

The most relevant document chunks are retrieved and supplied as context to the language model.

==================================================
6. PRODUCTION DESIGN
==================================================

A production document ingestion system can use the following architecture:

Controller
    |
    v
Document Processing Service
    |
    v
Format Processor
    |
    +---- PDF Processor
    |
    +---- JSON Processor
    |
    +---- TXT Processor
    |
    v
Extractor
    |
    v
Transformer
    |
    v
Embedding
    |
    v
Vector Store

The processing sequence should remain consistent:

Extract -> Transform -> Load

Format-specific behavior should remain inside the corresponding processor or extractor.

Common transformation and loading logic should be shared where possible.